"Cichacz's zero-sum partition conjecture for groups with one involution | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/365572/cichacz-s-zero-sum-partition-conjecture-for-groups-with-one)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # Cichacz's zero-sum partition conjecture for groups with one involution ... Sylwia Cichacz, “Disjoint zero-sum subsets in Abelian groups and its application – survey”, arXiv:2410.22245 (2024).\n\n# Cichacz's zero-sum partition conjecture for groups with one involution\n\nAbout 2 years old · traced to 2024\n\nLet $\\Gamma$Γ be a finite Abelian group of order $m$m, and let $I(\\Gamma)$I(Γ) be its set of involutions. Assume $|I(\\Gamma)|=1$∣I(Γ)∣=1, and put\n\n$$R=\\Gamma\\setminus\\bigl(\\{0\\}\\cup I(\\Gamma)\\bigr).$$R=Γ∖({0}∪I(Γ)).\n\nCichacz's zero-sum partition conjecture. For every positive integer $t$t and every integer partition $\\{m_i\\}_{i=1}^t${m i​}i=1 t​ of $m-2$m−2, with $m_i\\geq 2$m i​≥2 for every $i\\in[1,t]$i∈[1,t], there is a subset partition $\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^t${S i​}i=1 t​ of $R$R such that $|S_i|=m_i$∣S i​∣=m i​ and\n\n$$\\sum_{s\\in S_i}s=0$$s∈S i​∑​s=0\n\nfor every $i\\in[1,t]$i∈[1,t]. This extends the corresponding result known for cyclic groups; the paper states that the conjecture remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Sylwia Cichacz, “Disjoint zero-sum subsets in Abelian groups and its application – survey”, arXiv:2410.22245 (2024).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBounds in radial Moore graphs of diameter 3 - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X25001414)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; 10.1016/j.disc.2025.114533 ... In this paper we study upper bounds for both the maximum number of central vertices and the status of radial Moore graphs.\n\n## Discrete Mathematics\n\nDate: September 2025\n\nArticle: 114533\n\nVolume: Volume 348, Issue 9\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Bounds in radial Moore graphs of diameter 3\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Jesús M. Ceresuela, Nacho López\n\nShow more\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2025.114533\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nRadial Moore graphs preserve the order and the regularity of Moore graphs and allow some vertices to have more eccentricity than they should have in a Moore graph. One way to classify their resemblance with a Moore graph is the status measure. The status of a graph is defined as the sum of the distances of all pairs of ordered vertices and equals twice the Wiener index. Vertices with minimum eccentricity are called central vertices. In this paper we study upper bounds for both the maximum number of central vertices and the status of radial Moore graphs. Finally, we present a family of radial Moore graphs of diameter 3 that is conjectured to have maximum status.\n\n## Keywords\n\nRadial Moore graph\n\n;\n\nDegree/diameter problem\n\n;\n\nMoore bound\n\n;\n\nDiameter\n\n;\n\nStatus\n\n;\n\nWiener index\n\n## Data availability\n\nData will be made available on request.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn Sum-Free Functions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.10426)\nciteturn4academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; At the center of this new topic is a conjecture about the sum-freedom of the multiplicative inverse function $f_{\\text{\\rm inv}}(x)=x^{-1}$ (with $0^{-1}$ defined to be $0$). ... We also determine the ``right'' $q$-ary generalization of the binary multiplicative inverse function $f_{\\text{\\rm inv}}$ in the context of sum-freedom.\nTitle: On Sum-Free Functions\nAuthors: Alyssa Ebeling, Xiang-dong Hou, Ashley Rydell, Shujun Zhao\nDate: Mon Oct 14 12:22:29 2024\n\nA function from $\\Bbb F_{2^n}$ to $\\Bbb F_{2^n}$ is said to be {\\em $k$th order sum-free} if the sum of its values over each $k$-dimensional $\\Bbb F_2$-affine subspace of $\\Bbb F_{2^n}$ is nonzero. This notion was recently introduced by C. Carlet as, among other things, a generalization of APN functions. At the center of this new topic is a conjecture about the sum-freedom of the multiplicative inverse function $f_{\\text{\\rm inv}}(x)=x^{-1}$ (with $0^{-1}$ defined to be $0$). It is known that $f_{\\text{\\rm inv}}$ is 2nd order (equivalently, $(n-2)$th order) sum-free if and only if $n$ is odd, and it is conjectured that for $3\\le k\\le n-3$, $f_{\\text{\\rm inv}}$ is never $k$th order sum-free. The conjecture has been confirmed for even $n$ but remains open for odd $n$. In the present paper, we show that the conjecture holds under each of the following conditions: (1) $n=13$; (2) $3\\mid n$; (3) $5\\mid n$; (4) the smallest prime divisor $l$ of $n$ satisfies $(l-1)(l+2)\\le (n+1)/2$. We also determine the ``right'' $q$-ary generalization of the binary multiplicative inverse function $f_{\\text{\\rm inv}}$ in the context of sum-freedom. This $q$-ary generalization not only maintains most results for its binary version, but also exhibits some extraordinary phenomena that are not observed in the binary case.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn a conjecture by Sylwia Cichacz and Tomasz Hinc, and a related problem (https://publicatt.unicatt.it/retrieve/c3ec30e7-2ac4-44f6-99c2-75e5d03e0a47/2025--Morini-Pellegrini-Sora--On_a_conjecture.pdf)\nciteturn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Sylwia Cichacz and Tomasz Hinc introduced in [5] the concept of a Γ -magic rectangle set. ... Theorem 1.6 ([4, Theorem 3.9]). ... 16(2ℓ+ 1)(2h + 1), has more than one involution. ... the sum of the entries of each row and each column of A is zero, then we say that A is a zero-sum array.\nA related problem in graph theory involving row\nand column sums is discussed in [6].\nConsidering the existence of a Γ -magic rectangle set, Cichacz and Hinc proved the following result, where Υ denotes\nthe set of all finite abelian groups of odd order or containing more than one involution.\nTheorem 1.6 ([4, Theorem 3.9]). Let a, b > 1 be such that {a, b} ̸ = {2ℓ+ 1, 2α} where α , ℓ > 0. An MRSΓ (a, b; c) exists if\nand only if either a and b are both even or Γ ∈Υ .\nThey also proposed the following.\nConjecture 1.7 ([4]). Let a, b > 1. An MRSΓ (a, b; c) exists if and only if either a and b are both even, or Γ ∈Υ and\n{a, b} ̸ = {2ℓ+ 1, 2}.\nNote that, to prove the previous conjecture, one has to consider only the case {a, b} = {2ℓ+ 1, 2α}, where α , ℓ > 0.\nIn [5] the same authors proved that a necessary and sufficient condition for the existence of an MRSΓ (2ℓ+ 1, 4; 4h + 2)\nis that the abelian group Γ has more than one involution.\nIn this paper, we provide further evidences for the validity of Conjecture 1.7 proving, in particular, the following.\nProposition 1.8. Let α , ℓ, c be three integers with α ⩾2 and ℓ, c ⩾1. Let Γ ∈Υ be an abelian group of order (2ℓ+ 1)2αc.\nIf c is odd, then there exists an MRSΓ (2ℓ+ 1, 2α; c).\nTheorem 1.9. Suppose ℓ⩾1 and h ⩾0. An MRSΓ (2ℓ+ 1, 8; 4h + 2) exists if and only if the abelian group Γ , of order\n16(2ℓ+ 1)(2h + 1), has more than one involution.\nThe last section of the paper is devoted to constructions of Γ -magic rectangle sets whose elements are partially filled\narrays, that is, arrays where some cells are allowed to be empty.\n2. Notation and preliminary results\nGiven two integers a, b such that a ⩽b, we denote by [a, b] the set {a, a + 1, . . . , b} ⊂Z. If a > b, we set [a, b] = ∅.\nGiven an array (or, more in general, a partially filled array) A, we write E(A) for the list of its entries (considering their\nmultiplicities). Similarly, if T = {A1, A2, . . . , An} is a set of partially filled arrays, we write E(T) for E(A1) ∪ · · · ∪E(An). If\nthe sum of the entries of each row and each column of A is zero, then we say that A is a zero-sum array. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounting zero-sum subspaces for the multiplicative inverse function (https://arxiv.t3mt.com/html/2610.06445v1)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; arXiv:2610.06445v1 [math.NT] 05 Oct 2026 ... Kaimin Cheng Address: School of Mathematical Sciences, China West Normal University, Nanchong 637002, P. ... Combined with the known low-dimensional cases, the symmetry between dimensions $k$ and $n-k$, and the elementary middle-dimensional subfield construction, this proves a conjecture of Carlet: for every $3\\leq k\\leq n-3$, there exists a $k$-dimensional $\\mathbb{F}_{2}$-subspace $E$ such that $S(E)=0$. ... The inverse function is therefore a natural and particularly important example for the study of sum-freedom.\n\narXiv:2610.06445v1 [math.NT] 05 Oct 2026\n\n# Counting zero-sum subspaces for the multiplicative inverse function\n\nKaimin Cheng Address: School of Mathematical Sciences, China West Normal University, Nanchong 637002, P. R. China Email address: ckm20@126.com\n\n###### Abstract.\n\nLet $q=2^{n}$ with $n\\geq 6$, and let $\\mathbb{F}_{q}$ be the finite field with $q$ elements. For a subspace $E$ of $\\mathbb{F}_{q}$, define\n\n | $$S(E)=\\sum_{x\\in E\\setminus\\{0\\}}x^{-1}.$$  | \n\nLet $N_{n,k}$ denote the number of $k$-dimensional $\\mathbb{F}_{2}$-subspaces $E$ for which $S(E)=0$. We prove that, if $k\\geq 3$ and $n\\geq 2k+1$, then\n\n | $$\\left|\\frac{2^{n}N_{n,k}}{\\genfrac{[}{]}{0.0pt}{}{n}{k}_{\\!2}}-1\\right|<2^{r^{2}+r}2^{n(1-r/2)},$$  | \n\nwhere $r=\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\rfloor$, and $\\genfrac{[}{]}{0.0pt}{}{n}{k}_{\\!2}$ is the Gaussian binomial coefficient. In particular,\n\n | $$N_{n,k}\\sim 2^{-n}\\genfrac{[}{]}{0.0pt}{}{n}{k}_{\\!2}\\qquad(n\\to\\infty)$$  | \n\nuniformly for $n/3<k\\leq(n-1)/2$. Combined with the known low-dimensional cases, the symmetry between dimensions $k$ and $n-k$, and the elementary middle-dimensional subfield construction, this proves a conjecture of Carlet: for every $3\\leq k\\leq n-3$, there exists a $k$-dimensional $\\mathbb{F}_{2}$-subspace $E$ such that $S(E)=0$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn a conjecture about the sum-freedom of the binary multiplicative inverse function | DeepDyve (https://www.deepdyve.com/lp/springer-journals/on-a-conjecture-about-the-sum-freedom-of-the-binary-multiplicative-BCEjkw6Kfv)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last week; # On a conjecture about the sum-freedom of the binary multiplicative inverse function ... Designs, Codes and Cryptography·Apr 9, 2026\n# On a conjecture about the sum-freedom of the binary multiplicative inverse function\n\nHou, Xiang-dong; Zhao, Shujun\n\nDesigns, Codes and Cryptography·Apr 9, 2026\n\n## On a conjecture about the sum-freedom of the binary multiplicative inverse function\n\nHou, Xiang-dong; Zhao, Shujun\n\nDesigns, Codes and Cryptography, Volume 94 (4) – Apr 9, 2026\n\n### Abstract\n\nA recent conjecture by C. Carlet on the sum-freedom of the binary multiplicative inverse function can be stated as follows: for each pair of positive integers (n, k) with 3≤k≤n-3\\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}\\usepackage{amsmath}\\usepackage{wasysym}\\usepackage{amsfonts}\\usepackage{amssymb}\\usepackage{amsbsy}\\usepackage{mathrsfs}\\usepackage{upgreek}\\setlength{\\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}\\begin{document}$$3\\le k\\le n-3$$\\end{document}, there is a k-dimensional F2\\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}\\usepackage{amsmath}\\usepackage{wasysym}\\usepackage{amsfonts}\\usepackage{amssymb}\\usepackage{amsbsy}\\usepackage{mathrsfs}\\usepackage{upgreek}\\setlength{\\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}\\begin{document}$$\\mathbb F_2$$\\end{document}-subspace E of F2n\\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}\\usepackage{amsmath}\\usepackage{wasysym}\\usepackage{amsfonts}\\usepackage{amssymb}\\usepackage{amsbsy}\\usepackage{mathrsfs}\\usepackage{upgreek}\\setlength{\\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}\\begin{document}$$\\mathbb F_{2^n}$$\\end{document} such that ∑0≠u∈E1/u=0\\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}\\usepackage{amsmath}\\usepackage{wasysym}\\usepackage{amsfonts}\\usepackage{amssymb}\\usepackage{amsbsy}\\usepackage{mathrsfs}\\usepackage{upgreek}\\setlength{\\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}\\begin{document}$$\\sum _{0\\ne u\\in E}1/u=0$$\\end{document}. We confirm this conjecture when n is not a prime.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZero sum partition of Abelian groups into sets (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v25i1p20/pdf/)\nciteturn4search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.8 years ago; Zero sum partition of Abelian groups into sets ... Sylwia Cichacz ... property if and only if m ⩾3 and |Γ| is odd or Γ has more than one involution.\nZero sum partition of Abelian groups into sets\nof the same order and its applications\nSylwia Cichacz\nFaculty of Applied Mathematics\nAGH University of Science and Technology\nAl. Mickiewicza 30, 30-059 Krak´ow, Poland\ncichacz@agh.edu.pl\nSubmitted: Apr 19, 2017; Accepted: Jan 15, 2018; Published: Jan 25, 2018\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcations: 05C25, 05C78\nAbstract\nWe will say that an Abelian group Γ of order n has the m-zero-sum-partition\nproperty (m-ZSP-property) if m divides n, m ⩾2 and there is a partition of Γ into\npairwise disjoint subsets A1, A2, . . . , At, such that |Ai| = m and P\na∈Ai a = g0 for\n1 ⩽i ⩽t, where g0 is the identity element of Γ.\nIn this paper we study the m-ZSP property of Γ. We show that Γ has the m-ZSP\nproperty if and only if m ⩾3 and |Γ| is odd or Γ has more than one involution.\nWe will apply the results to the study of group distance magic graphs as well as to\ngeneralized Kotzig arrays.\nKeywords: Abelian group, zero sum partition, group distance magic labeling,\nKotzig arrays\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMore on the sum-freedom of the multiplicative inverse function | Designs, Codes and Cryptography | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10623-025-01712-x)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; # More on the sum-freedom of the multiplicative inverse function ...   * Published: 14 August 2025 ... We continue in the present paper the work made in [6] on the kth order sum-freedom of this function, which is important for the study of finite fields, and has applications in cryptography (it is used as a substitution box in many block ciphers, in particular the currently most important one: the Advanced Encryption Standard). ... The conjecture has been confirmed for \\(n\\le 12\\) [6] and for \\(6\\mid n\\) (as indicated above).\n### Conjecture 1.1\n\n[6] For even n, \\(F_{\\textrm{inv}}\\) is not kth order sum-free for \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\). For odd n, \\(F_{\\textrm{inv}}\\) is not kth order sum-free for \\(3\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n\nThe conjecture has been confirmed for \\(n\\le 12\\) [6] and for \\(6\\mid n\\) (as indicated above). In the present paper, we prove several new results concerning this conjecture. We find that, if \\(X^n+1\\) has a factor of the form \\(X^k+a_{k-1}X^{k-1}+\\cdots +a_2X^2+a_0\\in \\mathbb {F}_2[X]\\), then \\(F_{\\textrm{inv}}\\) is not kth order sum-free. Using the Lang-Weil bound on the number of zeros of absolute irreducible polynomials over finite fields, we prove that \\(F_{\\textrm{inv}}\\) is not kth order sum-free when \\(k\\ge 3\\) and \\(n\\ge 10.8k-5\\). We are also able to deduce that Conjecture 1.1 holds for all even n from a result in [6].\n\nThe rest of the paper is organized as follows: In Sect. 2, we study the companion matrix of the subspace polynomial of a k-dimensional subspace of \\(\\mathbb {F}_{2^n}\\). This leads to the conclusion that, if \\(X^n+1\\) has a factor of the form \\(X^k+a_{k-1}X^{k-1}+\\cdots +a_2X^2+a_0\\in \\mathbb {F}_2[X]\\), then \\(F_{\\textrm{inv}}\\) is not kth order sum-free. We then describe the values of k so that such a factor exists. Section 3 provides an alternative approach to Sect. 2 based on the Moore determinant. Not only does this new approach lead us to the same results of Sect. 2, but also it prepares us for the discussion in the next section. In Sect. 4, using the Lang-Weil bound, we show that when \\(k\\ge 3\\) and \\(n\\ge 10.8k-5\\), \\(F_{\\textrm{inv}}\\) is not kth order sum-free. The final section contains a short proof for Conjecture 1.1 with even n.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA random Hall-Paige conjecture | Inventiones mathematicae | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00222-025-01328-x)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; #### 6.3.1 The Cichacz conjecture ... It is well-known that abelian groups with just one involution (an order 2 element of \\(G\\)) have that \\(\\sum G\\neq 0\\), so a zero-sum partition in this case is impossible.\nVarious special cases of this problem were investigated by several authors, for example see [17] and all the references therein. In particular, our characterisation confirms the following conjecture of Cichacz [16].\n\n### Conjecture 1.6\n\nCichacz, [16]\n\nLet \\(G\\) be an abelian group with at least 3 involutions and suppose we have numbers \\(r_{1}, \\dots , r_{t}\\geq 3\\) with \\(r_{1}+\\cdots +r_{t}=|G|-1\\). Then there is a partition of \\(G\\setminus {e}=Z_{1}\\cup \\cdots \\cup Z_{t}\\) where each \\(Z_{i}\\) is a zero-sum set of size \\(r_{i}\\).\n\n#### 1.1.4 Harmonious groups\n\nGiven a group \\(G\\), not necessarily abelian, a harmonious ordering is an ordering of the elements of \\(G\\) as \\(a_{1},a_{2},\\ldots , a_{n}\\) such that \\(a_{1}a_{2}, a_{2}a_{3}, \\ldots , a_{n-1}a_{n}, a_{n} a_{1}\\) is also an ordering of the elements of \\(G\\) (i.e. the latter sequence contains no repetitions). Groups which have harmonious orderings are called harmonious. Harmonious groups were first introduced in 1991 by Beals, Gallian, Headley, and Jungreis [10]. They gave a characterisation of all abelian harmonious groups. Evans asked for a complete characterisation.\n\n### Problem 1.7\n\nEvans, [25]\n\nWhich finite groups are harmonious?\n\nIn Sect. 6.2, we give a characterisation of all sufficiently large harmonious groups. It turns out that a (large) group is harmonious if and only if it satisfies the Hall-Paige condition, and is not an elementary abelian 2-group.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn a Conjecture About the Sum-Freedom of the Binary Multiplicative Inverse Function (https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.21805)\nciteturn4academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Title: On a Conjecture About the Sum-Freedom of the Binary Multiplicative Inverse Function ... Carlet on the sum-freedom of the binary multiplicative inverse function can be stated as follows: For each pair of positive integers $(n,k)$ with $3\\le k\\le n-3$, there is a $k$-dimensional $\\Bbb F_2$-subspace $E$ of $\\Bbb F_{2^n}$ such that $\\sum_{0\\ne\\in E}1/u=0$.\nTitle: On a Conjecture About the Sum-Freedom of the Binary Multiplicative Inverse Function\nAuthors: Xiang-dong Hou, Shujun Zhao\nDate: Wed Apr 30 17:05:55 2025\n\nA recent conjecture by C. Carlet on the sum-freedom of the binary multiplicative inverse function can be stated as follows: For each pair of positive integers $(n,k)$ with $3\\le k\\le n-3$, there is a $k$-dimensional $\\Bbb F_2$-subspace $E$ of $\\Bbb F_{2^n}$ such that $\\sum_{0\\ne\\in E}1/u=0$. We confirm this conjecture when $n$ is not a prime.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFurther Results on Sum-Freedom of Binary and $q$-ary Functions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31489)\nciteturn4academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Fri Sep 25 16:31:17 2026 ... It is also known that when $k/n$ is not close to 0 or 1, the multiplicative inverse function on $\\Bbb F_{2^n}$ is not $k$th order sum-free. ... We formulate a conjecture about the 3rd o